PG.notes("l369", "<p class=\"summary\">First Ask-Me-Anything session (recorded for an earlier iOS course), answering general learner questions on how to absorb programming knowledge, getting a first dev job, studying, learning new tech, focus and daily routines. Nothing technical to build.</p>\n<h3>Learning and skill building</h3>\n<ul><li>Learn fundamentals first and surface features last; treat knowledge as a tree so flashy details have something to attach to.</li><li>Build your own small projects. Keep each one a minimal working version, then add features one at a time. Over-ambitious first projects tend to be abandoned.</li><li>Skills transfer between languages (variables, data types, functions), so a new language mostly means new syntax.</li><li>Searching well (Google, Stack Overflow, official docs) is a core professional skill; looking things up is normal.</li><li>When learning something new, map it onto what you already know, and schedule regular time for work outside your comfort zone.</li></ul>\n<h3>Career and study advice</h3>\n<ul><li>Without a portfolio, start on freelance marketplaces (Upwork, Fiverr and similar) to get real clients, real requirements and reviews; pay is low at first but raises as the track record grows.</li><li>At university prefer breadth and connections over chasing perfect grades.</li><li>Bias is not something you control; focus on factors you can change such as preparation, portfolio and work ethic.</li></ul>\n<h3>Focus and routine</h3>\n<ul><li>Protect attention: turn off notifications, and disconnect from the internet when it is not needed.</li><li>Reduce small daily decisions (clothes, breakfast) to save mental energy for work.</li><li>Her routine: short exercise, 20 minutes of meditation, journaling, then skimming tech news. Don't worry about missing days.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l370", "<p class=\"summary\">Second Ask-Angela-Anything session (recorded in 2017, with an iOS/Swift slant). Covers how much time to set aside for learning, where to practise coding between lessons, how the instructor picked up her programming languages, and some energy and focus habits. No code is written.</p>\n<h3>How much time to spend learning</h3>\n<ul><li>Aim for at least one hour a day on learning new tech, since the web ecosystem changes constantly and employers rarely give much paid learning time.</li><li>Protect your best-focus hour (for most people early morning) rather than the tired end of the day.</li><li>Match task type to energy: creative or new-concept work when sharp, routine admin when moderately sharp, rest when drained.</li><li>Hours alone are not the measure; practise on problems slightly beyond your current ability so you keep growing.</li></ul>\n<h3>Where to practise between lessons</h3>\n<ul><li>Project Euler: algorithm puzzles sortable by how many people solved them, solvable in any language.</li><li>CodeFights (now CodeSignal): arcade and challenge modes with automated tests and visible solutions after attempting.</li><li>The most valuable practice is building small original apps or sites with no step-by-step guide, practising reading docs, finding example code and using Stack Overflow.</li><li>Draw project ideas from your own annoyances, and try a 'new small site every day' habit (e.g. a notepad, egg timer, music player).</li></ul>\n<h3>Learning many programming languages</h3>\n<ul><li>Once one language is learnt, others mostly differ in syntax; concepts such as protocols and interfaces carry over.</li><li>Her own order was Visual Basic, C, C++, Objective-C, Swift, then JavaScript when clients wanted websites.</li><li>JavaScript feels permissive compared with strictly typed languages (e.g. out-of-range array access does not throw).</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The tool and website recommendations date from 2017; check they still exist and are current.</li><li>Health and diet comments (sugar, protein shakes, caffeine) are personal experience, not advice.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l371", "<p class=\"summary\">Q&amp;A episode with no code. Covers a staged route into freelancing, the instructor's daily work routine, native versus cross-platform mobile development, and a few productivity tools.</p>\n<h3>Starting as a freelancer</h3>\n<ul><li>Keep the day job at first; grow into freelancing gradually to limit risk.</li><li>Stage 1: set aside roughly 15 hours a week for a personal project that is genuinely useful to at least one person (not a hello-world or API experiment), deploy it, and treat it as portfolio material.</li><li>Stage 2: add roughly another 12-15 hours a week on small paid gigs from marketplaces such as Fiverr, Upwork or oDesk. Aim for experience, testimonials and references rather than income; keep early jobs under about a week.</li><li>Good starter work is what bigger agencies skip: bug fixes, updates, maintenance, small improvements.</li><li>Clear, regular communication with clients is the habit most likely to make or break a freelance career; repeat clients and word of mouth follow.</li></ul>\n<h3>Daily schedule ideas</h3>\n<ul><li>Spend a protected block (about 1.5 hours) at your personal best time of day learning something new.</li><li>Put high-concentration creative work next, lighter tasks (editing, client meetings) later in the day.</li><li>Check email in one fixed slot rather than reacting to notifications; switching notifications off is the simple protection.</li><li>Walking meetings and matching tasks to your energy level are suggested for productivity.</li></ul>\n<h3>Native vs cross-platform apps</h3>\n<ul><li>Native pros: best performance, strongest for animation and graphics, full access to platform APIs (push notifications, HealthKit), more community help.</li><li>Native cons: a separate Android build means a near-total rewrite, so more cost, time and developers.</li><li>Cross-platform tools (PhoneGap, Cordova, React Native) let web developers reuse JavaScript skills; React Native sits in the middle but sometimes needs custom native code for standard features, and its licence terms were seen as a drawback.</li><li>Conclusion: no blanket rule; judge each project's needs.</li></ul>\n<h3>Tools mentioned</h3>\n<ul><li>Product Hunt for discovering products; Duet (turn an iPad/iPhone into a second screen); Alfred (faster Mac launcher than Spotlight); Momentum (new-tab dashboard with to-do and focus task); CheatSheet (hold Cmd to list an app's shortcuts).</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Quitting a job to freelance before building a portfolio and client base is the main risk called out.</li><li>Taking large, ambitious first gigs instead of small ones.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l372", "<p class=\"summary\">Recording of a live livestream Q&amp;A covering learning strategy, getting hired, building a portfolio, reading documentation and course-roadmap news. No code is taught; the takeaways are career and study advice.</p>\n<h3>Learning and languages</h3>\n<ul><li>Treat programming languages as tools rather than school subjects: pick up whichever one the current project needs instead of mastering one fully first. An iOS app goal does justify getting solid in Swift first.</li><li>Mixing up syntax when switching languages is normal; lean on a good code editor and its linting/syntax highlighting for the mechanical part and spend your own effort on logic.</li><li>Learn by defining a concrete goal, splitting the topic into the parts that goal needs, then practising those parts repeatedly. Avoid trying to memorise a whole reference (every method of an API).</li><li>Information is cheap to look up, so skip memorising documentation. Search for a worked example (often Stack Overflow), spot the method used, then read the official docs for background as needed. Documentation quality varies a lot between vendors.</li></ul>\n<h3>Getting a developer job</h3>\n<ul><li>Competition exists for any desirable role; focus on the goal and on becoming good enough to be hard to ignore rather than watching other applicants.</li><li>Think from the hiring manager's side: they receive huge numbers of applications and have little time, so visible proof of ability beats listing qualifications.</li><li>Build a portfolio of real projects that go beyond tutorial material. A suggested idea is to clone a tool a manager uses daily (a task board like Trello, for example) and add your own feature that addresses a pain point, so the reviewer instantly grasps its value.</li><li>Apply the same perspective-taking to any goal that involves other people.</li></ul>\n<h3>Building projects and starting out</h3>\n<ul><li>Moving from tutorials to independent building requires struggle; the 100 Days of Code course deliberately reduces hand-holding over time (fewer videos, more docs and Google use) to build that independence, ending in your own portfolio projects.</li><li>Start small and grow a project step by step rather than aiming at a huge product at once.</li><li>For a startup there is no perfect moment: find spare time alongside current work, and expect chances to come from earlier preparation.</li><li>Young learners can try freelance marketplaces: charge very low prices first to gather reviews and experience, then raise the rate as demand grows.</li><li>Involve UI/UX design from the start of a project, not at the end; try many apps and note which experiences you like.</li><li>Pricing software: depends on appeal and niche size; broad-appeal products can be cheaper, niche ones need higher prices to cover development time.</li></ul>\n<h3>Course roadmap notes (as of the stream)</h3>\n<ul><li>No separate new iOS course planned; the existing iOS course was to receive updates, with a gradual shift toward SwiftUI while UIKit stays the job-relevant skill.</li><li>No Java course planned. Python and JavaScript are both viable for web work; the instructor sees no single 'most powerful' language.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Content is conversational and time-sensitive (2020 context); the iOS/SwiftUI remarks may be out of date.</li><li>Nothing here is required for the coding curriculum.</li></ul>");
